Subject: Make an RGB ColorBuffer linear on a GPU that keeps an app's fast clears per device

On an Intel Core i3-1005G1 (UHD Graphics, device 0x8A56, driver 31.0.101.2134, Vulkan
"Intel-0.404.2134", the last driver Intel ships for Ice Lake) every app window showed its new
frame's text and icons over parts of earlier frames: the Settings search bar twice, 205 px
apart (where it stood before a scroll), a collapsed toolbar's title over the list, and in Clock
blue blocks around the drawing of a black screen. `adb screencap` showed the same, so the
ColorBuffer's memory held it, not the task window. Rows are never sheared or shifted sideways,
which rules out a row pitch mismatch; whole blocks keep an earlier frame. That is a fast clear:
the app clears its window buffer through an image on its own VkDevice, the driver marks the
cleared blocks in that image's compression state instead of writing memory, and the renderer's
copy into the task window, screencap's readback and SurfaceFlinger, which read the same memory
through images on other devices, take it as it lies. The same build on Intel Arc (101.9150)
and an NVIDIA RTX 4050 is right.

Now on Intel's Gen9 to Gen11 GPUs (dsa_color_buffer_tiling.h lists them by PCI device ID), an
RGB ColorBuffer is linear whenever the device can draw into, sample, copy and blit it linear
with the same uses an optimal one has, at its size and with exported memory. A linear image has
no compression state, so every clear reaches memory. The renderer says once per format which
tiling it chose. Every other GPU keeps optimal ColorBuffers.

Every image the guest makes on a ColorBuffer's memory takes the ColorBuffer's tiling: an
AHardwareBuffer's through 0032's colorBufferTiling, now told the size, and a window buffer's
(VkNativeBufferANDROID), which upstream left at the guest's optimal even for a host-visible,
linear ColorBuffer. A linear window buffer image is also made at the ColorBuffer's size, since
a linear image's row pitch follows its width and a narrower image would read every row from
the wrong place.

